---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'support', label: 'What supports it' },
   { id: 'record', label: 'Record a session' },
   { id: 'permissions', label: 'macOS permissions' },
   { id: 'troubleshoot', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
   { id: 'privacy', label: 'Privacy' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Record a session"
   summary="Capture a real VoiceOver session to a .mov file or a real NVDA session to an .mp4 file. Pair the video with the session transcript when a bug report needs to show what happened, not just what was announced."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="support">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Support</p>
            <h2>What can be recorded</h2>
         </div>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row">
               <code>voiceover</code>
               <span>macOS. Records to a <code>.mov</code> path through native video capture.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="route-row">
               <code>nvda</code>
               <span>Windows. Records to an <code>.mp4</code> path.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="route-row">
               <code>virtual</code>
               <span>Not supported. The virtual reader runs with no window to capture. It produces transcript data instead.</span>
            </div>
         </div>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">No audio</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            On macOS a11ied captures video only. It does not request microphone access, so the file
            has no audio track.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="record">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Recording</p>
         <h2>Record a session</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Pass <code>--recording</code> with an output path when you start the session. The file
         closes when you stop it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover --recording ./recordings/checkout.mov http://localhost:3000/checkout --json
a1 sr next --json
a1 sr checkpoint "submitted the form" --json
a1 sr transcript --json
a1 sr stop --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         Recording covers the whole session, so keep sessions short and scoped to one behavior. Add
         <code>sr checkpoint</code> calls to mark the moments you want a reviewer to look for.
      </p>
      <p>
         Stopping writes the Markdown transcript next to the recording. For
         <code>./recordings/checkout.mov</code> that file is
         <code>./recordings/checkout.transcript.md</code>, and it carries an ISO timestamp per
         phrase, so a reviewer can match what the reader said to the point in the video.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="permissions">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">macOS</p>
         <h2>Grant Screen Recording to the right app</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         macOS grants screen capture to the application that runs the command, not to a11ied. If you
         run <code>a1</code> from Terminal, Terminal needs the permission. If your editor or agent
         runs it, that app needs it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor</code></pre>
      <p>
         On macOS, <code>doctor</code> runs a short native recording probe for the current host app
         and reports the result in the VoiceOver target's notes. Run it from the same app you plan to
         test from.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="troubleshoot">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Fixes</p>
         <h2>When recording fails</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Works in Terminal, fails in your editor</strong>
            <span>Grant Screen Recording to the editor in System Settings, then restart it.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>The command fails before recording starts</strong>
            <span>Run <code>npx playwright install chromium</code>. A browser-backed step is failing first.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>The target reports requires-setup</strong>
            <span>Run <code>a1 setup</code>, which runs the Guidepup steps <code>doctor</code> lists under Action items and re-checks afterward.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>The file is empty</strong>
            <span>Stop the session with <code>sr stop</code>. The recorder finishes the file at teardown.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="privacy">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Privacy</p>
         <h2>What ends up in the file</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         A recording captures the screen, which includes the page content, the browser window, system
         menus, and anything else visible during the run. Use test data, not real accounts, and check
         the file before attaching it to a ticket.
      </p>
      <p>
         A recording documents one run against one build. Record the route, the browser, the target,
         and the artifact path alongside it, or the file loses its meaning within a week.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
